{
  "topic_name": "Key Encryption Key (KEK) Cache Implementation and Security",
  "text": "The Key Encryption Key (KEK) cache is implemented as a thread-safe, fixed-size array (g_slots) that manages KEKs associated with specific principals. It employs an eviction policy based on Time To Live (TTL) and cache capacity, ensuring that active (non-expired) entries are never evicted even when the cache is full, while expired entries are eligible for replacement and securely cleared using OPENSSL_cleanse to prevent sensitive data leakage. The system incorporates concurrency control via pthread_mutex_lock and ensures process forking safety by invalidating cache entries upon fork to prevent stale key usage in child processes. This infrastructure supports credential management modules, such as those for git authentication, where credentials are dual-wrapped under user and server KEKs and retrieved via server-side unwrapping.",
  "citations": [
